        "text": "Where we're at (topographic contour/stream extraction)\n\nBuilt scripts/extract_vector_layers.py: extracts contour lines + stream\npaths directly from each plan's own raster (Data/mapTiles), independent\nof the old raster-diff \"topographic\" layer in samothrace-tiles (which\nturned out unreliable -- see the TopoCompare widget this all grew out\nof). Outputs real vector GeoJSON (2D LineString + elevation_m property,\nmatching gdal_contour's own convention) to Data/mapFeatures/.\n\nWhat's working:\n- Index (5m-interval) contours extract and label well. Classification\n  pipeline: local stroke-width threshold -> skeletonize -> cut at\n  branch points -> length filter separates real contour arcs from\n  digit/symbol strokes. Elevation labels read via OCR (ocrmac, macOS\n  Vision, no external binary needed), gated by \"must be a multiple of\n  5m\" -- calibrated against every real hit on the site, much more\n  reliable than OCR confidence or text-cleanliness alone.\n- Blue dashed stream lines extract cleanly (Actual State Plan only --\n  Restored has none marked).\n- A human-review loop now closes end-to-end: extraction queues genuine\n  ambiguity (not auto-accepted, not silently discarded) ->\n  ContourLabelReview widget shows the human the exact cropped image ->\n  apply_label_review.py persists the decision by geographic position\n  (survives fragment-id churn across re-runs) -> extraction respects it\n  forever after. First real answer (the \"09\"/9m misread -- actually a\n  contour loop/switchback, not a label) applied and verified.\n- TopoCompare widget: 4 panels (side-by-side sources, red/cyan overlay,\n  combined raster-diff result, extracted vectors), collapsible\n  sections, TOC sidebar, zoom/comment modes, and an edit mode for\n  manually deleting false positives / merging fragments that should be\n  one line -- corrections stored separately from the extractor's own\n  output (*.corrections.json) so re-running the extractor never\n  clobbers human review work.\n- Actual State Plan has several rounds of human corrections applied via\n  the widget (digit-label false positives deleted, broken fragments\n  reconnected). Restored State Plan has none yet.\n\nKnown limitations (see extract_vector_layers.py's own docstring for the\nfull list): contours get cut at every crossing rather than reconstructed\ncontinuous; the survey-boundary line can leak in as a false contour;\ncorrections key off array index, which can shift on a re-run (now at\nleast partially mitigated for label review via position-keyed\ndecisions, but *.corrections.json deletes/merges still don't have that\nprotection).\n\nNext thing to try: the 1m minor contours\n\nConfirmed visually (topo-compare-log.json entry #4) that the ~4 unlabeled\nlines between each pair of index contours are a genuinely distinct,\nvisibly thinner stroke weight in the source raster -- real 1m contours,\nnot extraction noise. We don't currently do anything with that\ndistinction: minor contours just come out unlabeled, like any other\nunlabeled fragment, with no elevation at all.\n\nA first attempt at measuring stroke width per-fragment (local\ndistance-transform average) didn't cleanly separate index vs. minor --\nin dense hillside areas the measurement gets confounded by *neighboring*\nlines, not just a fragment's own pen width, so ~93% of fragments bottom\nout at the same minimum value regardless of true drawn weight. Dropped\nrather than shipped on shaky statistical ground.\n\nWant to take another pass at this -- possibly a smarter width-measurement\napproach (perpendicular-to-tangent sampling instead of raw distance\n-transform?), or leaning on the fact that minor contours are regularly\nspaced between two known index contours (could interpolate if contour\nordering/adjacency were tracked topologically). This feels like exactly\nthe kind of fuzzy, \"does this look bold or thin to you\" judgment call\nthat a small human-review UI (same shape as the contour-label-review\nloop: a handful of targeted questions, not a wall of them) could help\ncalibrate or directly resolve, rather than trying to nail a fully\nautomatic classifier up front."
      },
